WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.920
"The Tomorrow" War is a topsy-turvy time travel
adventure starring Chris Pratt fighting aliens.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.220
From the fate of its heroes to the future
of mankind, here's the ending of "The Tomorrow

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.220
War" explained.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.630
When we meet him, Dan Forester is obsessed
with becoming the scientist he'd always hoped

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.550
to be, making his family proud in the process.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.500
When that plan doesn't work out, Dan begins
to spiral.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.730
He eventually learns that his insecurity over
his path splinters his family ... before his

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.880
own death in an accident in 2030.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.180
For Dan, the future he made for himself — the
one that began the night he was told he didn't

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.030
get his dream job — was a bleak one.

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:39.690
"You just, you never seemed happy with your
life."

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.730
But fighting in the future war, returning
safely, and defeating the White Spikes before

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:48.050
they could spread seems to have turned Dan's
outlook around, if not his whole life.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.720
The movie doesn't tell us exactly what his
future holds.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.309
We have no idea if he's able to parlay his
heroics into getting his dream job after all,

00:00:55.309 --> 00:01:00.420
or even if he manages to survive past 2030
in the new future he's helped to carve out.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.580
But the good news for Dan is that he's finally
found the real purpose and meaning in his

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.149
life, thanks to losing his daughter in the
future.

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:11.520
For however much time he has left, Dan is
focused on his family, and that seems to be

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.520
enough.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.399
Though he jumps into the future and then back
into the present, Dan Forester's "Tomorrow

00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:19.180
War" story is actually fairly straightforward.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:23.759
The same cannot be said for his daughter,
Muri Forester, who grows up to become a respected

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:28.429
and tireless military research scientist and
ends up working with her dad in an effort

00:01:28.429 --> 00:01:29.630
to save the world.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:33.920
We follow Dan for the whole film, but we see
Muri in fragments, and her ultimate fate in

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.700
the film means her future is rather ... complicated.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.810
We know that during her childhood, Muri was
very interested in science, so it makes sense

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.889
that she grew into a key researcher in the
fight for humanity's future.

00:01:45.889 --> 00:01:50.119
But by the end of "The Tomorrow War," future
Muri isn't just dead — the future she was

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:53.009
a part of has been changed by her father's
heroism.

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:56.969
The last time we see her in the film, it's
as a child, still full of hope and promise.

00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:00.770
As with Dan, we don't know if Muri's future
still holds these things.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.230
The film doesn't tell us what she grows up
to be after the future war is wiped away.

00:02:05.381 --> 00:02:09.689
"The Tomorrow War" ends with the future everyone
has accepted being wiped away, leaving plenty

00:02:09.689 --> 00:02:13.040
of unanswered questions for our heroes and
for the world at large.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.269
Many of these unanswered questions are positive
things, leaving open endless possibilities

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:21.689
now that humanity is no longer going to be
wiped out in 30 years by an alien horde.

00:02:21.689 --> 00:02:26.379
But one question might linger over at least
one character like a terrifying eventuality.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:31.080
When he meets the future version of his daughter,
Dan learns that he and his wife, Emmy, eventually

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.720
divorce, in part because of his own personal
insecurities throughout the 2020s.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.849
When he arrives back in the present, recommitted
to his family, Dan seems resolved to keep

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:44.590
that from happening by being a devoted father
and husband, but can he ever really know that

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:45.590
he'll prevent it?

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:48.010
What if he and Emmy are just destined to have
a finite marriage?

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:51.410
What if some other disappointment comes along
to make him spiral again?

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:55.530
Dan can work as hard at being a husband and
father as he wants, but some things might

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.150
be inevitable ... or not.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.360
Early in "The Tomorrow War," we see Emmy Forester
using her own skills and expertise to help

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.969
in the war effort, serving as a trauma counselor
for veterans who've returned from their seven-day

00:03:06.969 --> 00:03:08.689
tour of duty in the future.

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:12.439
Though they survived their fight with the
White Spikes, many of these people are missing

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:15.469
limbs, and the psychological damage runs even
deeper.

00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:19.670
It's a major mental health crisis amid the
larger crisis of the battle for the future.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:23.629
By the end of the film, the fight for the
future is seemingly over, but that doesn't

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:26.909
change the experiences of the veterans who
made it back to their present.

00:03:26.909 --> 00:03:30.459
The battle is over, and for those in the future,
it never even happened.

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:33.569
But for the soldiers who survived, the scars
will remain forever.

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:37.659
In a world eager to move on from crisis, will
those veterans be taken care of?

00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:41.209
Hopefully, people like Emmy will keep fighting
for them because their wounds didn't vanish

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:42.349
when the White Spikes did.

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:47.030
Early on in "The Tomorrow War," as the human
race of the present is just learning to comprehend

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:51.170
the future threat and the time-travel system
that warned them about it, Dan and his new

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.019
friend, Charlie, make a discovery.

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:57.029
All the soldiers who've been sent back to
train them for future battle are quite young,

00:03:57.029 --> 00:04:01.129
so young that they haven't been born yet when
the film kicks off, presumably to avoid some

00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:03.819
kind of paradox in which they run into their
past selves.

00:04:03.819 --> 00:04:07.490
It's the same reason why soldiers from the
present who are sent to the future are all

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:09.680
people who will be dead by 2051.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.420
It just keeps things easier.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.091
"Why don't we just jump back earlier in the
war?"

00:04:15.091 --> 00:04:17.169
"The jump link doesn't work like that."

00:04:17.169 --> 00:04:21.669
This system makes sense, but as the film ends,
it leaves a few lingering questions, particularly

00:04:21.669 --> 00:04:25.050
since the time-travel mechanism in the future
is destroyed.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.000
What happens to those future soldiers?

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.069
Since the future has presumably been changed
by destroying the White Spikes, it's possible

00:04:31.069 --> 00:04:33.960
they were never born at all, so do they just
... vanish?

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.910
It's one of the most fascinating wrinkles
in the film's world-building.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:41.639
Late in the film, after he returns to the
present and finds himself cut off from the

00:04:41.639 --> 00:04:45.800
future, Dan goes looking for the origins of
the White Spikes in the hopes of preventing

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.699
the future war from ever happening.

00:04:47.699 --> 00:04:51.880
With help from Charlie and one of his brightest
students, he realizes that the White Spikes

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:56.060
went undetected not because they were stealthy
but because they actually landed on Earth

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:57.340
centuries in the past.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:02.590
In fact, they were frozen in Russian glaciers
for 1,000 years until the ice finally thawed

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.030
out and freed them in the late 2040s.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:09.620
This means that climate change and the continued
thawing of polar ice around the globe contributed

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:13.520
to the awakening of the White Spikes and helped
cause the invasion.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.310
Humanity didn't know it, but by failing to
address the Arctic melting, they sealed their

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:18.310
own fate.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:22.270
This development is essentially presented
as a throwaway explanation in the overall

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.350
plot, but it leaves the human race with some
major future wisdom that they perhaps didn't

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:27.350
have before.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.960
The White Spikes may be gone, but the ice
is still melting, and that creates future

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.680
danger of a very different kind.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.430
Word of the future war arrives quickly, without
warning ... and with a lot of catching up

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.840
to do on the part of humanity in the present
day.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.040
One day, everyone's enjoying a soccer match,
and the next, the whole world has to prepare

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.270
for a war 30 years in the future.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.800
In the universe of "The Tomorrow War," we
learn that once proof of the future threat

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.800
is established, the world's governments leap
into action.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.129
Countries band together, uniting against a
common enemy on a scale never before seen

00:05:57.129 --> 00:06:01.919
in human history, and institute a global draft
to ensure there are enough soldiers to battle

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:02.960
the White Spikes.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.349
It's a massive effort, but what happens when
the future war ends?

00:06:06.349 --> 00:06:10.771
As the film closes, the human race is celebrating
the end of the White Spike threat, but they're

00:06:10.771 --> 00:06:12.560
also facing other issues.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.479
The present has endured massive loss of life
from the war, and there's also the ongoing

00:06:16.479 --> 00:06:18.820
collective trauma of the survivors.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:22.659
Families are shattered, incomes lost, entire
communities ravaged.

00:06:22.659 --> 00:06:26.870
It's a good time for the world to remain united
even though the larger threat is gone.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.539
The question is ... will they?

00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:33.080
The White Spikes, the menacing aliens that
dominate "The Tomorrow War," are set up as

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:34.770
seemingly perfect predators.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:38.849
They're heavily armored, fast, strong, and
they even have offensive weapons in the form

00:06:38.849 --> 00:06:40.639
of the spikes they shoot from their limbs.

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:44.150
They spread across continents in a matter
of months, overrunning the world through a

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:47.370
combination of aggression and a strong reproductive
cycle.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:51.930
They do all this after sitting in stasis under
ice for a millennium, and just one small colony

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:55.080
manages to reproduce and conquer an entire
planet.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.550
So, even though that colony has been wiped
out, the governments of the world have to

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:02.550
be wondering, where did the White Spikes come
from, and how do we stop them from trying

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:03.889
to take over the planet again?

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:06.300
Are they on other planets in our solar system?

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.270
Is there a chance we could encounter them
again?

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.950
Could more colonies still be in stasis somewhere
in the world?

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:15.069
There are a lot of lingering questions about
these fierce predators, and the world needs

00:07:15.069 --> 00:07:16.069
answers.

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:19.930
When Dan and his team arrive at the White
Spikes crash site near the end of the film,

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:23.590
they discover a crashed spaceship, but it
doesn't belong to the Spikes.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.000
No, those fierce aliens were actually cargo.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.470
The ship itself was piloted by a pair of more
humanoid-looking aliens who've since died

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:32.830
but were very clearly in charge of the ship.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:36.280
Sadly, because they have to blow the whole
vessel up to stop the White Spikes, Dan and

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:40.990
company don't get to preserve these alien
bodies, but their very existence poses a number

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:41.990
of questions.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:43.939
How did they crash 1,000 years ago?

00:07:43.939 --> 00:07:46.800
Was Earth their target, or did they end up
here accidentally?

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.229
Were they using the White Spikes as weapons,
or were they for something else?

00:07:50.229 --> 00:07:52.910
Are there more of them out there already on
the way to our planet?

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:57.020
Whatever they are, more than one alien species
poses a potential threat by the end of "The

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:00.330
Tomorrow War," and they might just be biding
their time.

